        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(slack): keep inbound message preview truncation UTF-16 safe",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\r\n\r\nFixes an issue where Slack messages containing emoji near the 160-character boundary produce an invalid `preview` string in the `PreparedSlackMessage` shape returned by `prepareSlackMessage`.\r\n\r\nWhen an inbound Slack message is prepared for dispatch, a 160-character preview is built from the raw message body by collapsing whitespace and calling `.slice(0, 160)`. Emoji and supplementary-plane characters use two UTF-16 code units (a surrogate pair). A cut that lands between the two halves of a surrogate pair produces a lone high surrogate — invalid Unicode — in the `preview` field that is stored on the `PreparedSlackMessage` object and passed to the dispatch pipeline.\r\n\r\n## Why This Change Was Made\r\n\r\nReplace `.slice(0, 160)` with `truncateUtf16Safe(..., 160)`, which detects and avoids splitting surrogate pairs. The preceding `.replace(/\\s+/g, \" \")` is unchanged. `truncateUtf16Safe` from `openclaw/plugin-sdk/text-utility-runtime` is the established helper for this class of fix throughout the codebase.\r\n\r\n## User Impact\r\n\r\nLong Slack messages containing emoji that straddle the 160-character preview boundary produce a `PreparedSlackMessage` with an invalid `preview` string. Any consumer of that field — system event labels, monitoring, or debug output — receives malformed Unicode. After this fix the `preview` field is always a valid Unicode string regardless of message content.\r\n\r\n## Evidence\r\n\r\n- **`prepare.ts:1182`** (post-fix): `const preview = truncateUtf16Safe(rawBody.replace(/\\s+/g, \" \"), 160);`\r\n- `preview` is a named field in `PreparedSlackMessage` (typed in `types.ts:38`) and returned from the function, so it flows into whatever the dispatch layer uses it for.\r\n- **Fix:** `truncateUtf16Safe(..., 160)` — same cap, surrogate-safe.\r\n- **Diff:** one new import, one call-site replacement in `prepare.ts`; surrogate-boundary regression in `prepare.test.ts`.\r\n- `truncateUtf16Safe` is already imported in the sibling file `approval-handler.runtime.ts` in the same Slack extension, confirming the import path is correct.\r\n\r\n### Behavior proof\r\n\r\n#### 1) Node runtime boundary reproduction (exact `prepare.ts` logic, no mocks, no Vitest)\r\n\r\nReplays the shipped preview pipeline on the surrogate boundary input (`159 × 'a' + 🐱`):\r\n\r\n```text\r\n=== Slack inbound preview UTF-16 reproduction (Node runtime, prepare.ts logic, no mocks) ===\r\nNode: v22.22.0\r\nrawBody code units: 161\r\ncollapsed code units: 161\r\nold .slice(0, 160) code units: 160\r\nold preview has unpaired surrogate: true\r\nold preview last char: U+D83D\r\ntruncateUtf16Safe(collapsed, 160) code units: 159\r\nfixed preview has unpaired surrogate: false\r\nfixed preview equals 159 ASCII a's: true\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis shows the before/after contract on the exact call shape in `prepare.ts`: whitespace collapse first, then surrogate-safe truncation to 160 code units.\r\n\r\n#### 2) Call-site regression through production `prepareSlackMessage`\r\n\r\n```text\r\n$ pnpm test extensions/slack/src/monitor/message-handler/prepare.test.ts -t \"truncates inbound preview\" --reporter=verbose\r\n\r\n ✓ slack prepareSlackMessage inbound contract > truncates inbound preview on a surrogate boundary\r\n\r\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\r\n      Tests  1 passed | 112 skipped (113)\r\n```\r\n\r\nRegression input: inbound body `\"a\".repeat(159) + \"🐱\"` → `prepared.preview` length 159, no unpaired surrogate. This exercises the real `prepareSlackMessage` path that sets `PreparedSlackMessage.preview`.\r\n\r\n**Proof gap:** No redacted live Slack workspace transcript in this validation environment. The Node reproduction and `prepareSlackMessage` regression cover the production preview builder; optional supplemental proof is a redacted inbound Slack message log showing a long emoji-near-boundary preview stays well-formed.\r\n\r\n- [x] AI-assisted (Cursor / Claude Sonnet 4.6)\r\n- [x] I understand what the code does\r\n- [x] Change is focused and does not mix unrelated concerns\r\n",
